From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Jul 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] armchina-npu: drop IRQF_ONESHOT from request_irq (no
 threaded handler)

IRQF_ONESHOT is valid only for request_threaded_irq() with a real
threaded handler. request_irq() wraps to request_threaded_irq(irq,
handler, NULL, flags, name, dev) with thread_fn=NULL, so IRQF_ONESHOT
trips a WARN at kernel/irq/manage.c:1502 during __setup_irq on every
boot (confirmed live on Orion O6N, 2026-07-18, armchina_aipu_probe ->
aipu_create_irq_object).

Driver does not use a threaded IRQ -- it uses a workqueue (INIT_WORK +
create_singlethread_workqueue), so IRQF_ONESHOT is inappropriate.
Driver's own comment calls this out: "Flag IRQF_ONESHOT is used when
sharing interrupts with other devices using thread_irq. Remove it if
you have no such a usage scenario." This follows that guidance.

Same fix as recipes-kernel/cix-modules/cix-npu-kmd-1.0's
0007-armchina-npu-drop-IRQF_ONESHOT.patch (validated there 2026-05-23:
WARN disappears from probe, IRQ still shared via IRQF_SHARED, still
uses workqueue bottom half) -- that fix only ever landed in the
standalone out-of-tree module recipe, never backported to this
in-tree next-patches series that builds the LTS kernel's baked-in
armchina_npu.ko. This backports it here.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/misc/armchina-npu/aipu_irq.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/misc/armchina-npu/aipu_irq.c b/drivers/misc/armchina-npu/aipu_irq.c
index 0000000..0000000 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/armchina-npu/aipu_irq.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/armchina-npu/aipu_irq.c
@@ -68,7 +68,7 @@
 	 * Remove it if you have no such a usage scenario.
 	 */
 	ret = request_irq(irqnum, aipu_irq_handler_upper_half,
-			  IRQF_ONESHOT | IRQF_SHARED | IRQF_PROBE_SHARED,
+			  IRQF_SHARED | IRQF_PROBE_SHARED,
 			  description, irq_obj->dev);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dev, "request_irq failed: irqnum %u, ret %d", irqnum, ret);
--
